(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-privacy"]={"plain":{"bills":{"privacy":{"slug":"privacy","title":"The Personal Privacy Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-0-1","heading":"\u00a7 0-1. Short title","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":"This Act may be cited as the Personal Privacy Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-0-2","heading":"\u00a7 0-2. Findings","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":"**(1)** Consent, as the operating mechanism of privacy law, has been measured and has failed. Of consent interfaces examined on the ten thousand most visited websites in the United Kingdom in 2020, fewer than 12 percent met the minimum legal requirements of the law that required them; the choices people make about tracking are substantially affected by small decisions in the design of the consent interface; and, in a study of an online travel intermediary subject to an opt-in requirement, the consumers who consented were trackable for a longer period of time.\n\n**(2)** The compliance apparatus of a consent-based privacy law is regressive. After the European Union's General Data Protection Regulation took effect, the profits of technology firms targeting European consumers fell about 2.1 percent, with the main burden on smaller companies, and websites were relatively more likely to keep the leading web technology vendors, raising concentration in that vendor market by 17 percent. The increase arose predominantly among vendors that use personal data and from the shares of vendors owned by two large advertising firms, and, although the overall increase faded by the end of 2018, the effect persisted among advertising vendors. A rule that burdens data a firm must obtain from others while sparing data it already holds favors the firm that already holds the most.\n\n**(3)** Corporate conduct has changed only where the law drew a categorical line. A statute in one State prohibiting the collection of biometric identifiers without consent, enforceable by the person whose identifier was taken, caused a facial-recognition vendor to be permanently restricted from selling its product to private parties and caused device makers to withhold identifying features from that State; a flat prohibition on advertising to minors based on profiling, and on the combination of personal data across services, changed the conduct of the largest platforms after years of consent-procedure enforcement had not.\n\n**(4)** The collection of personal data by devices has been documented at scale: a television manufacturer sold second-by-second viewing histories of its customers; an automobile manufacturer collected driving telemetry at intervals of seconds and sold it to data brokers who resold it to insurers; a voice-assistant provider retained the voice recordings of children indefinitely against requests for their deletion. No Federal statute addressed any of that conduct as such.\n\n**(5)** A data broker compiles, from sources with which the person has no relationship, a record of that person's location, associations, health, finances, and beliefs, and sells it to any buyer. The person cannot learn what the record contains, cannot correct it, and, under the law in force, cannot require that it be deleted. Registration of brokers without a mechanism for deletion has been tried in three States and changed little; registration with a single mechanism reaching every registered broker at once has been tried in one and is in use.\n\n**(6)** The Federal wiretap statute exempts interception by a party to a communication or with the consent of a party. A provider that operates the service through which a communication is made is, under that exemption, treated as a party to every communication made through it, and a third party whose code the provider embeds receives the contents of those communications without interception under the statute. The protection of the contents of communications has therefore migrated to the statutes of the several States, with the result that the same conduct is actionable in some States, not actionable in others, and in no State governed by a rule written for it.\n\n**(7)** A rule keyed to a quantity the regulated party controls will be satisfied by controlling that quantity. A firm designs the consent flow, sets the default, and writes the terms of service. What the firm does not control is what the person asked for. The obligations of this Act are therefore keyed to the product or service the person requested, and consent is confined to a narrow and revocable license that enlarges no prohibition.\n\n**(8)** The collection, processing, retention, and transfer of personal data is a class of economic activity conducted in and affecting interstate commerce, and the prohibitions of this Act address it as such."},{"anchor":"sec-0-3","heading":"\u00a7 0-3. Definitions","part":"SECTION 0 \u2014 SHORT TITLE, FINDINGS, DEFINITIONS","body":"**(a) Individual.** An **individual** is a natural person who resides in the United States, or a natural person, wherever resident, concerning whom covered data is collected, processed, retained, or transferred in the United States or by a covered entity.\n\n**(b) Covered data.** **Covered data** is information that identifies, or is linked or reasonably linkable, alone or in combination with other information, to an individual or to a device that identifies or is linked or reasonably linkable to one or more individuals, and includes derived data, inferences, and unique persistent identifiers. Covered data does not include de-identified data, or publicly available information except as \u00a7 1-5 provides. Whether information is covered data is determined without regard to the label the holder gives it and without regard to whether the holder has attached a name to it.\n\n**(c) Sensitive covered data.** **Sensitive covered data** is covered data that consists of or reveals any of the following, and covered data collected, processed, retained, or transferred for the purpose of identifying any of the following:\n\n1. a government-issued identifier not required by law to be displayed in public;\n2. the past, present, or future physical or mental health, disability, diagnosis, condition, or treatment of an individual, including reproductive and sexual health and gender-affirming care;\n3. genetic information;\n4. biometric information;\n5. a financial account number, card number, or any code, password, or credential permitting access to an account;\n6. precise geolocation information, meaning information that reveals the past or present location of an individual or device within a radius set in the schedule to this Act;\n7. the contents of a private communication, or information identifying the parties to, or the transmission of, a private communication, unless the covered entity is an intended recipient of the communication;\n8. account or device log-in credentials;\n9. the sexual behavior or sexual orientation of an individual;\n10. calendar, address book, and contact information, and photographs, audio recordings, and video maintained for private use;\n11. a visual depiction of the naked or undergarment-clad private area of an individual;\n12. the video programming, video content, books, or other expressive material requested, viewed, or selected by an individual;\n13. race, color, ethnicity, national origin, religion, union membership, citizenship, immigration status, or transgender or nonbinary status;\n14. an online activity profile, meaning covered data identifying the activity of an individual or device over time and across websites, online services, or applications not under common branding, collected or used to evaluate, analyze, or predict the individual's behavior or characteristics;\n15. information about an individual the covered entity knows or should know is under 18 years of age;\n16. status as a member of the Armed Forces; and\n17. neural data, meaning information generated by measuring the activity of an individual's central or peripheral nervous system.\n\n**(d) De-identified data.** **De-identified data** is information that cannot reasonably be used to infer or derive the identity of an individual and is not linked or reasonably linkable to an individual or device, regardless of whether it is aggregated, where the person holding it takes reasonable technical, physical, and administrative measures to ensure that it cannot be used to re-identify any individual or device; publicly commits to process, retain, and transfer it only in de-identified form and not to attempt to re-identify it, except as necessary and limited to test the effectiveness of those measures; and contractually obligates every recipient to the same commitments and to require them of every subsequent recipient. **In any proceeding, the person holding or transferring the information bears the burden of establishing that it is de-identified.** Information that has been re-identified, and information as to which the holder has not taken the measures this subsection requires, is covered data from the time it was collected.\n\n**(e) Publicly available information.** **Publicly available information** is information that a covered entity has a reasonable basis to believe has been lawfully made available to the general public from Federal, State, Tribal, or local government records, in accordance with any restriction the government entity placed on it; from widely distributed media; from a website or online service available to all members of the public, whether for a fee or without one; from a disclosure to the public required by law; or from the visual observation of the physical presence of an individual or device in a public place, not including data collected by a device in the individual's possession. Information is not available to all members of the public if the individual to whom it pertains restricted it to a specific audience, maintained a default setting restricting it to a specific audience, **or made it public by a default setting the individual did not choose.** Publicly available information does not include an obscene visual depiction; biometric information; genetic information unless the individual to whom it pertains made it public; an inference drawn from public sources that reveals sensitive covered data; sensitive covered data made available by a data broker; publicly available information combined with covered data; or an intimate image known to be nonconsensual.\n\n**(f) Collect; process; retain; transfer.** To **collect** covered data is to buy, rent, gather, obtain, receive, access, capture, or otherwise acquire it by any means. To **process** it is to perform any operation on it, including analyzing, organizing, structuring, using, modifying, combining, training a model on it, or otherwise handling it. To **retain** it is to store, maintain, save, or otherwise keep it, in any form. To **transfer** it is to disclose, release, share, disseminate, make available, sell, rent, or license it to another person by any means, for consideration or for a commercial purpose.\n\n**(g) Sale.** A **sale** of covered data is a transfer for monetary or other valuable consideration, regardless of form, title, or characterization, and includes a transfer in exchange for a service, for data, or for a discount, and a transfer of access to data without a transfer of the data itself.\n\n**(h) Covered entity.** A **covered entity** is any person, other than an individual acting in a personal or household capacity, that alone or jointly with others determines the purposes or means of collecting, processing, retaining, or transferring covered data, and includes every person that controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with a covered entity. A Federal, State, Tribal, or local government entity is not a covered entity, and a person is not a covered entity to the extent it acts as a service provider to a government entity. **No threshold of revenue, size, or number of individuals is a condition of being a covered entity.**\n\n**(i) Service provider.** A **service provider** is a person that collects, processes, retains, or transfers covered data on behalf of and at the direction of a covered entity, or of another service provider, pursuant to a written contract meeting \u00a7 1-6(d). A person that determines, alone or jointly with others, the purposes or means of any collection, processing, retention, or transfer, or that acts outside the instructions of the covered entity, is a covered entity and not a service provider as to that data.\n\n**(j) Third party.** A **third party** is a person that receives covered data from a person other than the individual to whom it pertains and is not a service provider as to that data. Two persons under common control are not third parties to each other as to data an individual would reasonably expect them to share.\n\n**(k) Data broker.** A **data broker** is a covered entity that collects, processes, retains, or transfers covered data concerning individuals with which it has no direct relationship, where that data concerns more than the number of individuals set in the schedule to this Act in any 12-month period, or where revenue from it exceeds half of the entity's revenue in that period. A person is not a data broker to the extent it acts as a service provider.\n\n**(l) Unique persistent identifier.** A **unique persistent identifier** is an identifier reasonably linkable to an individual or to a device that identifies or is linked or reasonably linkable to one or more individuals, including a device identifier, an Internet Protocol address, a cookie, a beacon, a pixel tag, an advertising identifier, a customer number, a pseudonym, a probabilistic identifier, and a telephone number, and does not include an identifier assigned solely to give effect to an individual's choice under this Act.\n\n**(m) Advertisement; advertiser; advertising facilitator.** An **advertisement** is information disseminated to an individual, a device, or a group of individuals or devices to promote a product, service, or cause, whether provided by one person to another for dissemination for consideration or disseminated by a person to promote its own product or service. An **advertiser** is a person that, directly or indirectly, provides consideration for that dissemination, or that disseminates an advertisement for its own product or service. An **advertising facilitator** is a person that receives consideration to disseminate an advertisement and collects or processes covered data in connection with its dissemination. A natural person is neither, except to the extent engaged in a commercial activity that is more than de minimis.\n\n**(n) Target.** To **target** the dissemination of an advertisement is to perform, or cause to be performed, any computational process designed to select an individual, a device, or a group of individuals or devices to receive the advertisement on the basis of covered data pertaining to the individual, the device, or the members of the group. Targeting does not include a computational process undertaken solely to transmit, route, store, or provide connections for an advertisement through a system or network.\n\n**(o) Recognized place.** A **recognized place** is a State; Indian lands; a county, municipality, city, town, township, village, borough, or similar unit of general government incorporated under State law; a census designated place as defined by the Bureau of the Census; a designated market area as defined in section 122(j)(2)(C) of title 17, United States Code; or a congressional district. A recognized place does not include a subdivision of any of those, and does not include a ZIP Code. **Indian lands** means Indian country as defined in section 1151 of title 18, United States Code, and land in Alaska owned by a Native village or a Village Corporation under the Alaska Native Claims Settlement Act.\n\n**(p) Consent.** **Consent** is the license described in \u00a7 1-3, obtained in the manner that section requires, and has no other meaning in this Act. Agreement to terms of service, a privacy policy, or any other general instrument is not consent; continued use of a product or service is not consent; and inaction is not consent.\n\n**(q) Signal.** A **signal** is a setting of a browser, operating system, device, platform, or application that communicates, in a form a covered entity can receive by machine, an individual's choice under this Act.\n\n**(r) Sensor; captured sensor data.** A **sensor** is a microphone, a camera, a component that determines precise location, a component that measures a biological, physical, or physiological characteristic of a person, a component that measures the operation or movement of a vehicle or device, and any other component of a device that captures information about the physical world or about a person's body, voice, or movement. **Captured sensor data** is covered data produced by a sensor, including any inference drawn from it.\n\n**(s) Device.** A **device** is electronic equipment capable of collecting, processing, retaining, or transferring covered data that is used by one or more individuals, including a telephone, a computer, a television, a vehicle, a wearable, an appliance, a speaker, and any equipment connected to a network.\n\n**(t) Knowledge; should know.** A covered entity **knows** a fact where it has actual knowledge of it, and **should know** a fact where a reasonable and prudent person in its position, on the information available to it, would know it. Nothing in this Act requires a covered entity to collect additional covered data, or to verify age or identity, in order to acquire knowledge.\n\n**(u) Person; State.** A **person** includes an individual, a trust, a partnership, an association, an organization, a company, a corporation, and a governmental entity. A **State** includes the District of Columbia, the Commonwealth of Puerto Rico, and every territory and possession of the United States.\n\n**(v) Commission.** The **Commission** is the Federal Trade Commission."},{"anchor":"sec-1-1","heading":"\u00a7 1-1. The rule","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"**(a)** A covered entity may not collect, process, retain, or transfer covered data, and may not direct a service provider to do so, beyond what is necessary, proportionate, and limited to provide or maintain the specific product or service the individual to whom the data pertains requested, including the routine administrative, operational, and account-servicing activity that product or service entails, and a communication, other than an advertisement, that the individual would reasonably anticipate within the relationship; or to effect a purpose stated in \u00a7 1-2.\n\n**(b) The request is the measure.** What the individual requested is determined from what the individual did and asked for, and not from the covered entity's description of its product, its terms of service, its business model, or any purpose the covered entity assigns to the product after the fact. A feature the individual did not use, and a purpose the individual did not invoke, is not part of what the individual requested.\n\n**(c) Necessity is determined by the function.** Collection, processing, retention, or transfer is necessary to provide a product or service only where the product or service cannot be provided without it. That a product would be more profitable or more effective with the data does not make the data necessary to provide it. Personalization of the product or service the individual requested, presented within that product or service and used for no advertisement, is part of providing it where the individual has chosen it and may decline it by a setting as easy to reach as the setting that enables it; personalization the individual cannot decline is not.\n\n**(d) Burden.** In any proceeding, the covered entity bears the burden of establishing that a collection, processing, retention, or transfer was within subsection (a).","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-1-2","heading":"\u00a7 1-2. Permitted purposes","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"**(a) These and only these.** A covered entity may collect, process, retain, or transfer covered data previously collected in accordance with this Act, to the extent necessary, proportionate, and limited to the following purposes and no others:\n\n1. to initiate, manage, or complete a transaction the individual requested, including billing, shipping, delivery, storage, and accounting;\n2. to perform system maintenance and diagnostics; to develop, maintain, repair, or improve the product or service for which the data was collected; to conduct internal analytics to improve that product or service; and to debug or repair errors that impair its functionality, where the data is not used to contact, target, profile, or make a decision concerning any individual;\n3. to authenticate the users of a product or service;\n4. to fulfill a warranty or effect a product recall;\n5. to prevent, detect, protect against, investigate, or respond to a security incident, meaning an intrusion or trespass on a network, a system, or physical premises, a medical alert, a fire alarm, or an access-control event;\n6. to prevent, detect, protect against, investigate, or respond to fraud, harassment, or illegal activity, except that covered data may not be transferred to a government entity for consideration under this paragraph;\n7. to comply with a legal obligation imposed by Federal, State, Tribal, or local law, or to investigate, establish, prepare for, exercise, or defend a legal claim, or to comply with a warrant, subpoena, court order, or other lawful process;\n8. to prevent an individual from suffering death, serious physical injury, or a serious health risk, where the covered entity believes in good faith that the risk is imminent;\n9. to conduct a public or peer-reviewed scientific, historical, or statistical research project in the public interest that adheres to every law governing the protection of human subjects, limits any transfer of sensitive covered data to what the research requires, and transfers no covered data to a data broker;\n10. to deliver a communication, at the individual's direction, between the individual and another person;\n11. to transfer covered data to a person acquiring control of the covered entity's assets in a merger, acquisition, bankruptcy, or similar transaction, where the covered entity gives each affected individual advance notice naming the recipient, and a reasonable opportunity before the transfer to withdraw consent and to request deletion under \u00a7 6-1, and where the recipient is bound by this Act as to that data as though it had collected it;\n12. to process covered data so that it becomes de-identified data; and\n13. to comply with this Act, including to maintain a record of an individual's choices for the sole purpose of giving effect to them.\n\n**(b) Closure.** The purposes stated in subsection (a) are those and only those for which covered data may be collected, processed, retained, or transferred beyond \u00a7 1-1(a). No other purpose is supplied by any source, and the omission of any purpose from subsection (a) is a determination of Congress and not a gap. In particular, no purpose is supplied by the covered entity's legitimate interest, its business needs, the compatibility of a use with a disclosed purpose, or the reasonable expectations of a hypothetical consumer.\n\n**(c) The purpose does not travel.** A purpose in subsection (a) permits the collection, processing, retention, or transfer it names and nothing further. Covered data collected for a purpose stated in subsection (a) may not thereafter be used for any purpose not itself stated in subsection (a) or in \u00a7 1-1(a).\n\n**(d) Training a model is not a purpose.** The training, fine-tuning, or evaluation of a model, algorithm, or system on covered data, other than a model that serves the same product or service back to the individuals whose data trained it and to no one else, is not within \u00a7 1-1(a) and is not a purpose stated in subsection (a). It is lawful only on de-identified data, or under a consent meeting \u00a7 1-3 that names it."},{"anchor":"sec-1-3","heading":"\u00a7 1-3. Consent: a license and nothing more","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"**(a) What consent may do.** For covered data that is not sensitive covered data, and for a use that is not prohibited by Section 2, a covered entity may collect, process, retain, or transfer covered data beyond \u00a7\u00a7 1-1(a) and 1-2 for a specific purpose to which the individual has consented. Consent enlarges nothing else. It does not enlarge Section 2, does not enlarge Section 3, and does not permit any collection, processing, retention, or transfer of sensitive covered data that \u00a7 2-6 does not permit.\n\n**(b) How consent is obtained.** Consent is obtained only by an affirmative act of the individual, made in response to a request that \u2014\n\n1. is presented separately from any terms of service, privacy policy, or other instrument, through the primary medium in which the product or service is offered;\n2. states the specific purpose, the specific categories of covered data, the specific persons to whom the data will be transferred, and the period for which it will be retained;\n3. states that the individual may refuse and may revoke, and that neither refusal nor revocation affects the product or service;\n4. presents the option to refuse at least as prominently as the option to accept, and requires no more steps to refuse than to accept;\n5. is made in each language in which the product or service is offered and in a manner accessible to individuals with disabilities; and\n6. is not made in a manner \u00a7 2-7 prohibits.\n\n**(c) Consent is specific and does not accumulate.** Consent obtained for one purpose is not consent for another. A single request may not seek consent for more than one purpose. A request may not be presented to an individual who has refused, or who has revoked, before the period set in the schedule to this Act has elapsed.\n\n**(d) Consent is revocable.** A covered entity shall provide a means to revoke consent that is as easy to use as the means by which consent was given, available at all times, and requiring no contact with any person. On revocation the covered entity shall stop the collection, processing, retention, and transfer the consent permitted, and shall delete the data so collected or process it into de-identified data, within the period set in the schedule to this Act, and shall notify every service provider and third party to which it transferred the data, each of which shall do the same.\n\n**(e) Consent is not transferable.** A third party that receives covered data under a consent is bound by the purpose and the period of that consent, and may not rely on it for any other purpose. A consent may not be assigned, licensed, or sold.\n\n**(f) No conditioning.** A covered entity may not condition, or attempt to condition, access to a product or service, the price of a product or service, the quality, level, or functionality of a product or service, or participation in a loyalty, rewards, or discount program, on the giving of consent, and may not offer a discount, a payment, or any other thing of value for it. A loyalty, rewards, or discount program may collect and process the covered data that operating the program requires under \u00a7 1-1(a); it may not require consent under this section as a condition of participation. **A price, a tier, or a feature that is available only to an individual who has consented, or that is unavailable only to an individual who has refused or revoked, is a condition prohibited by this subsection regardless of how it is described.**\n\n**(g) Consent obtained otherwise is not consent.** An agreement obtained in a manner this section does not permit, or in a manner \u00a7 2-7 prohibits, is not consent for any purpose under this Act, and the covered entity holds the data as though no consent had been given."},{"anchor":"sec-1-4","heading":"\u00a7 1-4. Retention and deletion by default","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"**(a)** A covered entity shall delete covered data, or process it into de-identified data, when it is no longer necessary for the purpose under \u00a7 1-1(a) or \u00a7 1-2 for which it was collected, and shall maintain and adhere to a retention schedule stating, for each category of covered data, the period for which it is retained and the basis for that period. Deletion means rendering the data permanently unreadable, indecipherable, and unrecoverable.\n\n**(b)** Captured sensor data is governed by \u00a7 2-5, and biometric information may not be retained beyond the earlier of the point at which the purpose for which it was collected has been served and the period set in the schedule to this Act after the individual's last interaction with the covered entity."},{"anchor":"sec-1-5","heading":"\u00a7 1-5. Publicly available information","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"**(a) Outside this Section.** Publicly available information is not covered data for purposes of this Section and of Section 6.\n\n**(b) Inside Sections 2 and 4.** Publicly available information is covered data for purposes of Sections 2 and 4, and of every provision of Section 7 enforcing them. A prohibition in Section 2 applies to covered data without regard to its source, and a data broker's duties under Section 4 apply to publicly available information the broker holds.\n\n**(c) Combination.** Publicly available information that is combined with covered data, and any inference drawn from it that reveals sensitive covered data, is covered data for every purpose of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-1-6","heading":"\u00a7 1-6. Service providers and third parties","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"**(a) A service provider follows instructions.** A service provider shall collect, process, retain, and transfer covered data only on the documented instructions of the covered entity and only to the extent necessary, proportionate, and limited to the service the covered entity requested; shall not collect, process, retain, or transfer covered data it knows or should know the covered entity obtained or uses in violation of this Act; shall assist the covered entity in responding to an individual's exercise of a right under this Act; shall delete or return all covered data at the end of the service unless retention is required by law; and shall engage another service provider only under a written contract binding it to this subsection, with notice to the covered entity.\n\n**(b) No combination across clients.** A service provider may not combine covered data it receives from or on behalf of one covered entity with covered data it receives from or on behalf of another, or with covered data it collects from its own interaction with an individual, except as necessary for a purpose stated in \u00a7 1-2(a)(3), (5), (6), or (7). This subsection applies regardless of any contract, and a contract permitting such combination is void to that extent.\n\n**(c) A third party is bound by the purpose.** A third party may collect, process, retain, or transfer covered data it receives only for the purpose for which it was transferred to it, which must itself be a purpose permitted by this Act as to the covered entity that transferred it, and is bound by this Act as though it had collected the data from the individual.\n\n**(d) Contract.** A person may act as a service provider only under a written contract that states the instructions, the nature and purpose of the processing, the categories of data, the duration, and the rights and obligations of each party; that prohibits the combination subsection (b) prohibits; and that relieves neither party of any obligation under this Act.\n\n**(e) Successive actors.** A covered entity or service provider that transfers covered data in compliance with this Act is not liable for a violation by the recipient of which it had neither knowledge nor reason to know at the time of transfer; a person that knows or should know that a recipient is violating or about to violate this Act shall cease transferring to it. A person that collects, processes, retains, or transfers covered data in compliance with this Act is not liable for a violation by the person from which it received the data."},{"anchor":"sec-1-7","heading":"\u00a7 1-7. Material changes","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"A covered entity that changes its collection, processing, retention, or transfer of covered data in a way that would likely affect an individual's decision to provide covered data to it shall give each affected individual advance notice, and a means to refuse the change as to that individual's data, and shall not apply the change to the covered data of an individual who refuses unless the change is necessary to provide the product or service the individual requested."},{"anchor":"sec-1-8","heading":"\u00a7 1-8. What this Section does not reach","part":"SECTION 1 \u2014 WHAT MAY BE COLLECTED","body":"**(a) Journalism.** Nothing in this Act limits the gathering, preparing, collecting, photographing, recording, writing, editing, reporting, publishing, or investigating of news or information concerning matters of public interest for dissemination to the public, or the processing of covered data necessary to it.\n\n**(b) Personal and household activity.** Nothing in this Act applies to an individual's collection, processing, retention, or transfer of covered data in the course of a personal or household activity.\n\n**(c) Minors.** Nothing in this Act limits or relieves any obligation under the Children's Online Privacy Protection Act of 1998 or any other Federal law addressing individuals under 18 years of age, and where such a law and this Act both apply, the more protective governs."},{"anchor":"sec-2-1","heading":"\u00a7 2-1. Surveillance advertising prohibited","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a) The rule.** An advertiser or an advertising facilitator may not target the dissemination of an advertisement, and may not knowingly enable another person to target the dissemination of an advertisement, including by providing to that person a list of individuals or devices, contact information, a unique persistent identifier, or any other covered data that can be used to select a recipient.\n\n**(b) Consent does not enlarge this section.** Subsection (a) applies regardless of any consent, and no consent, agreement, term, or election by any individual permits targeting.\n\n**(c) What is not targeting.** Dissemination of an advertisement is not targeting where the advertisement is a contextual advertisement within \u00a7 2-2, or where the advertisement is a communication within \u00a7 2-4.\n\n**(d) Regardless of form.** Subsection (a) applies to any computational selection of recipients on the basis of covered data, regardless of the form, title, or characterization of the process, of the party performing it, or of the data, and applies whether the selection is performed by the advertiser, by the facilitator, by a service provider to either, by a third party, or on the individual's device by software that any of them provides or controls.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-2-2","heading":"\u00a7 2-2. Contextual advertising","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a) The inputs are closed.** An advertisement is a **contextual advertisement** only where every input to the selection of its recipient is one of the following, and no other:\n\n1. the content the individual is viewing or with which the individual is engaging at the time of dissemination, or the query the individual has entered, where the advertisement is disseminated in proximity to that content or in response to that query; and\n2. a recognized place associated with the individual or device at the time of dissemination.\n\n**(b) Any covered-data input disqualifies.** Selection that uses any covered data pertaining to the individual or device as an input, including an inference about the individual, a history of the individual's activity, a profile, a location more precise than a recognized place, or the content the individual viewed at any earlier time, is targeting and not contextual advertising, regardless of whether the covered data is combined with the inputs in subsection (a). Content that the covered entity or any other person selected for presentation to the individual by a process using covered data pertaining to the individual is not content within subsection (a)(1), and an advertisement selected on the basis of it uses a covered-data input.\n\n**(c) Delivery data may not be re-used.** Covered data collected in connection with disseminating a contextual advertisement, including the fact of its dissemination and the individual's response to it, may not be used to target the dissemination of any advertisement, may not be used to enable any person to do so, and may not be retained beyond the period necessary for measurement under \u00a7 2-3."},{"anchor":"sec-2-3","heading":"\u00a7 2-3. Measurement","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a) Aggregate measurement permitted.** Covered data may be processed and transferred to measure and report the frequency, reach, performance, and attribution of advertising only where every report is an aggregate report meeting subsection (b), and every individual-level record used to produce it is handled as subsection (c) requires.\n\n**(b) An aggregate report.** A report is an aggregate report only where \u2014\n\n1. it reports on a cohort of not fewer than the number of individuals or devices set in the schedule to this Act, and reports nothing about any cohort smaller than that number;\n2. it is produced not earlier than the delay set in the schedule to this Act after the last event it reports;\n3. each reported value is perturbed by random noise sufficient that the presence or absence of any single individual or device in the cohort cannot be determined from the report, alone or in combination with any other report; and\n4. reports concerning overlapping cohorts are produced no more frequently than the interval set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(c) Individual records.** An individual-level record linking an advertisement to an individual or device, or to an action of an individual, may be retained by a person only for the period necessary to produce the aggregate report and for no other purpose, may not be transferred to any person other than a service provider performing the measurement, and shall be deleted when the report is produced. No such record may be used to target, to profile, or to make any decision concerning an individual.\n\n**(d) Independent measurement.** A person that performs measurement under this section for another person is a service provider as to the data, is bound by \u00a7 1-6, and may not combine data across the persons for which it performs measurement except to produce an aggregate report meeting subsection (b) for each of them.\n\n**(e) Burden.** In any proceeding, the person producing or relying on a report bears the burden of establishing that it meets subsection (b)."},{"anchor":"sec-2-4","heading":"\u00a7 2-4. Communications a person requested","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a) The boundary.** A communication is not an advertisement targeted within \u00a7 2-1 where \u2014\n\n1. the individual requested, from the person sending it, communications of that kind, through the channel used, by an act meeting \u00a7 1-3(b), **or the communication meets subsection (a-1)**;\n2. the communication is sent by that person, or by a service provider acting for that person, and by no one else;\n3. the only covered data used to select the individual or the content is covered data the individual provided directly to that person in the course of the product or service the individual requested from it; and\n4. the individual may stop the communications by a means as easy as the means by which they were requested, and the request is honored within the period set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(a-1) A purchase satisfies subsection (a)(1), for that seller and nothing else.** Subsection (a)(1) is satisfied without an act meeting \u00a7 1-3(b) where \u2014\n\n**(A)** the individual provided the contact point used, to the person sending the communication, in the course of purchasing or negotiating to purchase a product or service from that person;\n\n**(B)** the communication promotes only products or services of that person, of a kind similar to the one the individual purchased or negotiated to purchase;\n\n**(C)** that person gave the individual, at the time the contact point was collected, a clear and conspicuous opportunity to decline communications of that kind, free of charge and without any other condition, and gives that opportunity in every communication sent under this subsection;\n\n**(D)** the individual has not declined; and\n\n**(E)** not more than the period set in the schedule to this Act has elapsed since the individual's most recent purchase from, or negotiation to purchase with, that person.\n\n**(a-2) What subsection (a-1) does not do.** Subsection (a-1) satisfies subsection (a)(1) and nothing else. **Subsections (a)(2), (a)(3) and (a)(4) apply unchanged**, so a communication under subsection (a-1) may be sent by no one but that person and a service provider acting for it, may use no covered data other than what the individual provided directly to it, and may be stopped as easily as the purchase was made. Subsection (a-1) does not permit the transfer or disclosure of a contact point to any other person, **including an affiliate, a parent, or a subsidiary**; does not apply to a contact point obtained from any person other than the individual; does not apply to a communication promoting the products or services of another person; and confers no permission that survives the individual declining under subparagraph (C).\n\n**(b) Not an exception.** Subsection (a) defines what is not targeting; it is not an exception to \u00a7 2-1, and a communication that does not meet every element of subsection (a) is targeting if it meets \u00a7 0-3(n). Covered data received from any other person, and any inference drawn from data other than the data the individual provided, disqualifies a communication under subsection (a)(3)."},{"anchor":"sec-2-5","heading":"\u00a7 2-5. The sensor rule","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a) Capture on invocation only.** A sensor of a device may capture information only while the individual is actively invoking the function the sensor serves, and only to the extent the function requires. A function is actively invoked by an act of the individual directed to it; it is not invoked by the device being powered, by the individual's presence, by the individual's use of another function, or by any setting the individual did not choose. The function a sensor serves is defined by what the individual asked the device to do, and not by the covered entity's description of the device, its features, or its purposes.\n\n**(b) Use only to fulfill.** Captured sensor data may be used only to perform the function the individual invoked, and may not be used to target the dissemination of an advertisement, to build or enrich a profile, to make an inference about the individual beyond what the function requires, or to be sold or otherwise transferred to any person other than a service provider performing the function.\n\n**(c) Deletion when the function is performed.** Captured sensor data shall be deleted within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the function is performed, unless the individual has directed that it be retained as a product of the function, such as a recording, a photograph, or a route the individual asked to keep.\n\n**(d) Wake words and readiness.** A device may process sensor input on the device, and only on the device, to detect an act of invocation, where no information so processed is retained, transferred, or used for any purpose other than detecting the invocation, and where the processing does not itself produce covered data. Information so processed is not captured sensor data.\n\n**(e) Service improvement.** A covered entity may retain captured sensor data to improve the function only under a consent meeting \u00a7 1-3 that names that purpose, is requested separately from any other consent, is not requested during or as a condition of the individual's invocation of the function, and may be revoked at any time; and only where the data is processed into de-identified data within the period set in the schedule to this Act after capture. No other consent enlarges this section.\n\n**(f) Vehicles and telemetry.** A vehicle, an appliance, and any other device that measures its own operation or movement captures covered data within this section, and information about the operation, location, or movement of a device ordinarily used by an individual is captured sensor data pertaining to that individual.\n\n**(g) Terms are not invocation.** Agreement to terms of service, a privacy policy, or any other instrument, and any setting, default, or configuration of the device not chosen by the individual by an act meeting \u00a7 1-3(b), is not an invocation, is not a consent, and does not enlarge this section.\n\n**(h) The device maker and the operator are both bound.** This section binds every covered entity that designs, manufactures, distributes, or operates the device or the function, and every service provider to any of them."},{"anchor":"sec-2-6","heading":"\u00a7 2-6. Sensitive covered data","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a) Collection and processing.** A covered entity may not collect or process sensitive covered data except where doing so is strictly necessary to provide or maintain the specific product or service the individual requested, or is strictly necessary to a purpose stated in \u00a7 1-2(a)(3), (5), (6), (7), (8), (9), or (13). Consent does not enlarge this subsection.\n\n**(b) No sale.** A covered entity, a service provider, and a third party may not sell sensitive covered data, and may not transfer it for consideration of any kind, regardless of purpose and regardless of any consent.\n\n**(c) No transfer.** A covered entity may not transfer sensitive covered data to a third party except as strictly necessary to a purpose stated in subsection (a), or under a consent meeting \u00a7 1-3 that names the recipient and the purpose, where the recipient is not a data broker and the transfer is not a sale.\n\n**(d) Government identifiers.** A covered entity may not collect, process, retain, or transfer a Social Security number except to facilitate an extension of credit, authentication, the detection or prevention of fraud or identity theft, the payment or collection of taxes, the enforcement of a contract between the parties, or as required by law.\n\n**(e) Inference is collection.** A covered entity that infers sensitive covered data from other covered data collects sensitive covered data within this section, and an inference is subject to this section as though the individual had disclosed the fact inferred."},{"anchor":"sec-2-7","heading":"\u00a7 2-7. Manipulation","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a) The practices.** In connection with any consent, choice, right, or setting under this Act, a covered entity may not \u2014\n\n1. present the option to accept more prominently, in larger type, in a more contrasting color, or in a more reachable position than the option to refuse;\n2. require more steps, more time, or more information to refuse, revoke, or opt out than to accept;\n3. present a request for consent more than once in the period set in the schedule to this Act, or present it during or as a condition of the individual's use of a function;\n4. use a preselected or default choice to obtain consent;\n5. describe the consequence of refusing in terms that are false, misleading, or that state a consequence \u00a7 1-3(f) prohibits;\n6. condition the dismissal of a request on acceptance, or make refusal unavailable from the screen on which acceptance is available;\n7. obtain, by any interface, a choice from an individual who could not reasonably know that a choice was being made;\n8. treat the closing, pausing, muting, or scrolling of content as a choice; or\n9. use an interface designed or modified with the purpose or the substantial effect of impairing an individual's ability to make the choice this Act gives the individual, where the interface is materially similar to a practice in paragraphs (1) through (8).\n\n**(b) Consequence.** A consent, choice, or agreement obtained through a practice in subsection (a) is void, is not consent, and confers nothing on the covered entity; and a right an individual attempted to exercise through an interface employing such a practice is deemed exercised."},{"anchor":"sec-2-8","heading":"\u00a7 2-8. De-identification and re-identification","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a)** No person may re-identify de-identified data, or attempt to, except as \u00a7 0-3(d) permits to test the measures that section requires; and no person may transfer de-identified data to a person it knows or should know intends to re-identify it.\n\n**(b)** A person that holds data it asserts to be de-identified bears the burden, in any proceeding, of establishing that it meets \u00a7 0-3(d)."},{"anchor":"sec-2-9","heading":"\u00a7 2-9. Consent does not enlarge this Section; anti-substitution","part":"SECTION 2 \u2014 THE PROHIBITIONS","body":"**(a)** No prohibition in this Section is enlarged, relaxed, or waived by any consent, agreement, term, election, or setting, and a term purporting to do so is void.\n\n**(b)** Each prohibition in this Section applies to the conduct it describes regardless of the form, title, or characterization the covered entity gives the conduct, of the technology used, of whether the conduct is performed directly or through a service provider, a third party, an affiliate, or a person outside the United States, and of whether the covered data is characterized as de-identified, pseudonymous, aggregated, modeled, or inferred where it does not meet \u00a7 0-3(d)."},{"anchor":"sec-3-1","heading":"\u00a7 3-1. The signal is binding","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ONE SIGNAL","body":"**(a)** A covered entity that receives a signal shall treat it as the individual's exercise of every choice under this Act that the signal communicates, including a refusal or revocation of consent under \u00a7 1-3 and a request under \u00a7 4-2 where the signal so states, and shall give it effect without any further act by the individual.\n\n**(b) No authentication.** A covered entity may not require an individual to authenticate, to create an account, to log in, or to provide any information as a condition of honoring a signal, and may not disregard a signal on the ground that it cannot verify the identity of the individual.\n\n**(c) A signal set by default is a signal.** A signal is honored whether the individual set it or it was set as a default of the browser, operating system, device, platform, or application, provided the individual is able to change it. A covered entity may not disregard a signal on the ground that it was set by default.\n\n**(d) Form.** A covered entity may not decline to receive a signal transmitted in a form in general use for the purpose, and may not require that a signal be transmitted in a form of its own choosing.\n\n**(e) Conflict.** Where a signal conflicts with a consent the individual gave to the covered entity before the signal was received, the signal governs, and the covered entity may not ask the individual to confirm, restate, or reconsider the consent except by a single request meeting \u00a7 1-3(b), made not earlier than the period set in the schedule to this Act after the signal was received.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ONE SIGNAL","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-3-2","heading":"\u00a7 3-2. No re-prompting","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ONE SIGNAL","body":"A covered entity that has received a refusal, a revocation, or a signal from an individual may not present that individual with a request for the consent so refused or revoked, or any request to the same effect, before the period set in the schedule to this Act has elapsed, and may then present it once."},{"anchor":"sec-3-3","heading":"\u00a7 3-3. No banner as a condition","part":"SECTION 3 \u2014 ONE SIGNAL","body":"**(a)** A covered entity may not condition access to a product or service, or to any content, on an individual's responding to a request for consent, a notice of choices, or any similar interface, and may not display such an interface in a manner that obstructs the product, service, or content until the individual responds.\n\n**(b)** A covered entity is not required by this Act, or by any other law as applied to covered data, to display any interface requesting consent, and no consent is required for any collection, processing, retention, or transfer within \u00a7 1-1(a) or \u00a7 1-2."},{"anchor":"sec-4-1","heading":"\u00a7 4-1. Registration","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 DATA BROKERS","body":"**(a) Duty.** A data broker shall register with the Commission not later than the date set in the schedule to this Act, and annually thereafter, by filing a registration stating its legal name and every name under which it does business; its physical, electronic, and internet addresses and a contact person; the categories of covered data it collects, processes, retains, or transfers; the categories of persons to which it transfers covered data; whether it credentials the persons to which it transfers covered data and, if so, how; each incident of unauthorized access to covered data it held that it reported to any government entity in the preceding year and the number of individuals affected; and the address at which an individual may exercise a right under this Act, which shall be honored without a fee.\n\n**(b) Self-executing.** The duty to register arises by operation of this section on the facts stated in \u00a7 0-3(k). No form, determination, or action by the Commission is a condition of it, and a data broker that has not been contacted by the Commission is not excused.\n\n**(c) Notice.** A data broker shall display on every website and application it operates a clear statement that it is a data broker and a link to the registry under \u00a7 4-2.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 4 \u2014 DATA BROKERS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-4-2","heading":"\u00a7 4-2. The registry and the deletion mechanism","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 DATA BROKERS","body":"**(a) The registry.** The Commission shall maintain a publicly searchable registry of every data broker registered under \u00a7 4-1, publishing every element of each registration and the date it was filed. Publication is ministerial and shall occur within the period set in the schedule to this Act after filing.\n\n**(b) One mechanism.** The Commission shall maintain, as part of the registry, a mechanism by which an individual, or a parent or guardian on behalf of a minor, may by a single act direct every registered data broker to delete all covered data concerning the individual that the broker did not collect directly from the individual, including every inference; and to cease collecting covered data concerning the individual. The mechanism shall accept a request from an individual, from a person the individual authorizes, and from a signal, shall require of the individual only the information necessary to identify the data to be deleted, and shall be available without a fee. The Commission may use information submitted through the mechanism only to transmit the request to registered data brokers and to confirm to the individual that it was transmitted, may not retain it beyond the period necessary for that purpose, may not disclose it to any person other than a registered data broker for that purpose, and may not match it against any other record; and no other agency may obtain it.\n\n**(b-1) Expedited request on a threat.** **(1)** An individual who has been threatened, or who has reasonable grounds to believe that the individual or a member of the individual's household is at risk of violence, stalking, or harassment by a person seeking to locate the individual, may designate a request under subsection (b) as expedited. The designation is made by the individual's statement, given in the mechanism by the same act as the request, that the individual meets this paragraph. **No document, evidence, or other showing is required; neither the Commission nor any data broker may require one, inquire into the grounds, or decline or delay a request for want of one.**\n\n**(2)** A request submitted on an individual's behalf by a court, by a law enforcement agency, by a protective or security service of the United States or of a State, by a State program that keeps confidential the address of a victim of domestic violence, sexual assault, or stalking, or by an office providing the suppression of covered information under the Judiciary Integrity Act, the Executive Integrity Act, or the Congressional Integrity Act, is expedited without any statement.\n\n**(3)** The mechanism shall make an expedited request available to every registered data broker on receipt, and shall transmit with it that it is expedited and nothing concerning the grounds. A data broker may use the designation only to comply with the request, may not transfer it, and may not retain it after complying. The confirmation to the individual under subsection (b) shall state that the request was expedited and the time it was submitted.\n\n**(4) The designation is conclusive.** A request the mechanism transmits as expedited, and a request delivered as expedited under subsection (f), is expedited for every purpose of this Act. Whether the individual met paragraph (1), and whether the person submitting the request is a person named in paragraph (2), is not a defense in any action concerning compliance with the request and may not be inquired into in it.\n\n**(c) Duty of the broker.** A data broker shall retrieve requests from the mechanism at intervals not longer than the period set in the schedule to this Act, and shall comply with each request within the period set in the schedule to this Act after it was submitted, **or, for a request designated expedited under subsection (b-1), within 72 hours after it was submitted, whatever the interval at which the broker retrieves requests,** by deleting the data and by maintaining, for the sole purpose of giving effect to the request, a record of the request and the minimum data necessary to ensure that covered data concerning the individual is not thereafter collected or retained. A data broker shall not transfer any covered data concerning the individual after the request is submitted.\n\n**(d) Ministerial.** The maintenance of the registry, the publication of registrations, and the operation of the mechanism are ministerial duties of the Commission that involve no discretion, and are enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person. Their performance does not depend on any rule, and no rule may condition, limit, or delay them.\n\n**(e) Consumer reports.** A request under this section does not require the deletion of covered data to the extent it is used by a consumer reporting agency in a consumer report subject to the Fair Credit Reporting Act, and does not reach that use; it reaches every other use of that data by that agency.\n\n**(f) If the mechanism does not exist.** Until the mechanism under subsection (b) is in operation, a request delivered to a data broker at the address stated in its registration, or by a signal, has the effect of a request submitted through the mechanism, and the broker's duty under subsection (c) runs from delivery. A request so delivered is expedited where it states that the individual meets subsection (b-1)(1) or is delivered by a person named in subsection (b-1)(2)."},{"anchor":"sec-4-3","heading":"\u00a7 4-3. Prohibitions on brokers","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 DATA BROKERS","body":"**(a)** A data broker may not sell or transfer sensitive covered data, and \u00a7 2-6 applies to it without any exception under \u00a7 2-6(c).\n\n**(b)** A data broker may not transfer covered data to a person it knows or should know intends to use it to stalk, harass, threaten, defraud, or discriminate unlawfully against any individual, or to a person it has not identified, and may not advertise or offer covered data for any such use.\n\n**(c)** A data broker may not collect covered data concerning an individual who has submitted a request under \u00a7 4-2, and may not transfer covered data to a government entity in any manner the Data Warrant Act prohibits that entity from obtaining it; if that Act is not in effect, this subsection applies as though section 2714 of title 18, United States Code, as that Act would add it, were in effect."},{"anchor":"sec-4-4","heading":"\u00a7 4-4. Consequence","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 DATA BROKERS","body":"**(a) Failure to register.** A data broker that has not registered when required is liable to the United States for the amount set in the schedule to this Act for each day of the failure, increasing by the amount set in the schedule to this Act for each successive period of 30 days, without any cap, and the amount is determined by a court in an action by the Commission or by the attorney general of a State. Registration after the failure does not remit the amount accrued.\n\n**(b) Failure to delete.** A data broker that has not complied with a request under \u00a7 4-2 within the period that section requires is liable to the individual for the amount set in the schedule to this Act for each day of the failure, in an action under \u00a7 7-4, in addition to any other remedy."},{"anchor":"sec-4-5","heading":"\u00a7 4-5. Wherever processed","part":"SECTION 4 \u2014 DATA BROKERS","body":"This Section, and every prohibition of Section 2, applies to covered data concerning an individual wherever the data is collected, processed, retained, or transferred, and to a person outside the United States that collects, processes, retains, or transfers covered data concerning individuals in the United States. A person may not obtain, from a person outside the United States, a selection of recipients, a profile, or an inference derived from covered data that the person could not lawfully produce under this Act, and covered data transferred outside the United States remains subject to this Act in the hands of every recipient."},{"anchor":"sec-5-1","heading":"\u00a7 5-1. A provider is not a party by providing the service","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 INTERCEPTION","body":"Section 2511(2) of title 18, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(j)(1)** For purposes of paragraph (d), a person that provides the service, website, application, or facility through which a communication is made is not a party to the communication by reason of providing it, and does not acquire the consent of a party by reason of the party's use of it, except that acquisition of the contents of the communication that is necessarily incident to the rendition of the service the user requested, and that is used for no other purpose, is not an interception.\n>\n> **\"(2)** A person that acquires the contents of a communication by reason of software, code, a pixel, a tag, or any other component that another person has placed in or connected to a service, website, application, or facility is not a party to the communication and does not have the consent of a party to it by reason of the placement.\n>\n> **\"(3)** For purposes of paragraph (d), a party's consent to interception is given only by an act meeting section 1-3 of the Personal Privacy Act, and agreement to terms of service, a privacy policy, or any other general instrument, continued use of a service, and inaction are not consent.\"","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 5 \u2014 INTERCEPTION","body":"*Amends chapter 119 of title 18, United States Code.*"}]},{"anchor":"sec-5-2","heading":"\u00a7 5-2. Consent to disclosure","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 INTERCEPTION","body":"Section 2702(b)(3) of title 18, United States Code, is amended to read as follows, with the new matter marked as inserted:\n\n> **\"(3)** with the lawful consent of the originator or an addressee or intended recipient of such communication, or the subscriber in the case of remote computing service, **which consent is given only by an act meeting section 1-3 of the Personal Privacy Act and is not given by agreement to terms of service, a privacy policy, or any other general instrument;\"**"},{"anchor":"sec-5-3","heading":"\u00a7 5-3. Records divulged to private persons","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 INTERCEPTION","body":"Section 2702(c)(6) of title 18, United States Code, is amended to read as follows, with the new matter marked as inserted:\n\n> **\"(6)** to any person other than a governmental entity, **to the extent the Personal Privacy Act permits the provider to transfer the record to that person; or\"**"},{"anchor":"sec-5-4","heading":"\u00a7 5-4. Damages, and what is excluded from them","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 INTERCEPTION","body":"Section 2520 of title 18, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(h) Acquisition incident to a service.** No damages under subsection (c) are recoverable for the acquisition of information other than the contents of a communication, or for the acquisition of contents that is necessarily incident to the rendition of a service the user requested and is used for no other purpose, and the remedy for any such acquisition that violates the Personal Privacy Act is the remedy that Act provides.\""},{"anchor":"sec-5-5","heading":"\u00a7 5-5. Operative in any event","part":"SECTION 5 \u2014 INTERCEPTION","body":"**(a)** A person that acquires the contents of a communication made through a service, website, application, or facility, beyond what is necessarily incident to the rendition of the service the individual requested, or that transfers those contents to any person other than a service provider performing that service, violates this Act, and the acquisition is an interception for purposes of \u00a7 7-4, whether or not the amendments made by \u00a7\u00a7 5-1 through 5-4 take effect.\n\n**(b)** The acquisition of information other than the contents of a communication, and the acquisition of contents necessarily incident to the rendition of the requested service and used for no other purpose, is governed by Section 1 and not by this Section, and is not a violation for purposes of \u00a7 7-4(a)(4)."},{"anchor":"sec-6-1","heading":"\u00a7 6-1. The rights","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE INDIVIDUAL'S RIGHTS","body":"**(a)** An individual has the right, on request to a covered entity, to \u2014\n\n1. confirm whether the covered entity collects, processes, retains, or transfers covered data concerning the individual, and to access that data in a form a person can read and in a portable, structured, machine-readable form;\n2. learn the categories of sources from which the data was collected, the name of each third party and service provider to which it was transferred, and the purpose of each transfer;\n3. correct any inaccuracy or incompleteness in the data;\n4. delete the data, and every inference drawn from it; and\n5. have the data exported to the individual or to another person the individual designates, without restriction on its onward use.\n\n**(b) Notification runs with the data.** On a correction or deletion, the covered entity shall notify every service provider and third party to which it transferred the data, each of which shall correct or delete accordingly and shall notify its own transferees.\n\n**(c) Deletion of data obtained from others.** A covered entity that obtained covered data from a source other than the individual complies with a request under subsection (a)(4) by deleting the data and retaining only a record of the request and the minimum data necessary to ensure that the data is not thereafter collected or retained.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE INDIVIDUAL'S RIGHTS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-6-2","heading":"\u00a7 6-2. Exercise","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE INDIVIDUAL'S RIGHTS","body":"**(a) Timing.** A covered entity shall comply with a request, or state in writing why it declines and how the individual may appeal, within the period set in the schedule to this Act \u2014 and a statement that does not identify the paragraph of \u00a7 6-3(a) relied on and the facts supporting it does not satisfy this subsection \u2014 which may be extended once by the same period where the number or complexity of the individual's requests requires it and the individual is told why within the initial period.\n\n**(b) Cost.** A covered entity shall act on the number of requests per year set in the schedule to this Act without charge, and may charge a reasonable fee, disclosed in advance, for a further request in the same year. The receipt of a large number of requests does not itself make compliance impracticable.\n\n**(c) Means.** A covered entity shall provide a means to exercise each right that is as easy to use as the means by which the individual provides covered data, shall not require the creation of an account, and shall accept a request from a person the individual authorizes and from a signal.\n\n**(d) Verification.** A covered entity may require only the information reasonably necessary to verify that the request is made by or for the individual, may not use that information for any other purpose, and may not require verification of a request to refuse or revoke consent, to opt out, or to stop communications.\n\n**(e) Appeal.** A covered entity that declines a request shall provide a means of appeal as easy to use as the means of request, shall decide the appeal in writing with reasons within the period set in the schedule to this Act, and shall inform the individual how to complain to the Commission and to the attorney general of the individual's State."},{"anchor":"sec-6-3","heading":"\u00a7 6-3. Exceptions","part":"SECTION 6 \u2014 THE INDIVIDUAL'S RIGHTS","body":"**(a) These and only these.** A covered entity may decline a request, in whole or in part and with a written explanation, only where compliance would \u2014\n\n1. require it to re-identify de-identified data, or to retain data in identifiable form solely to be able to associate a request with it;\n2. require access to, or the correction or deletion of, the sensitive covered data of another individual;\n3. require the correction or deletion of data subject to a warrant, a subpoena, or a preservation order in a matter to which the covered entity is a party;\n4. violate a Federal, State, Tribal, or local law not preempted by this Act, or a professional ethical obligation;\n5. delete data necessary to perform a contract the individual has with the covered entity, for the period of the contract;\n6. delete data the covered entity reasonably believes is evidence of fraud or of a violation of law by the individual, for the period necessary to investigate or prosecute it;\n7. disclose a trade secret, where the covered entity identifies it and discloses everything else; or\n8. further fraud or threaten the security of a system, where the covered entity states the specific basis for that belief.\n\n**(b) Closure.** No other exception is supplied by any source, and the omission of any exception is a determination of Congress and not a gap. A covered entity that declines under subsection (a) shall comply with the remainder of the request."},{"anchor":"sec-7-1","heading":"\u00a7 7-1. Ministerial functions","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** The following are ministerial duties of the Commission, involve no discretion, are enforceable in an action in the nature of mandamus by any person, and do not depend on any rule, appropriation, or other act of any officer:\n\n1. the maintenance of the registry and the operation of the mechanism under \u00a7 4-2;\n2. the receipt and publication, within the period set in the schedule to this Act after filing, of every certification filed under subsection (b); and\n3. the maintenance of a public page describing each right and obligation under this Act in plain language and in the ten languages most used in the United States.\n\n**(b) Certification.** A covered entity that, in a calendar year, collects, processes, retains, or transfers the covered data of more than the number of individuals set in the schedule to this Act, or that is a data broker, shall file with the Commission, not later than the date set in the schedule to this Act in the following year, a certification signed by its chief executive officer stating that in that year it did not target the dissemination of any advertisement or enable any person to do so; did not sell sensitive covered data; did not collect, process, retain, or transfer captured sensor data except as \u00a7 2-5 permits; honored every signal it received; and complied with every request under Section 6 within the period \u00a7 6-2 requires; or stating, as to each, that it did not and describing the instances. A certification that omits any statement, or that states compliance in general terms without addressing each element, does not satisfy this subsection.\n\n**(c) The certification is evidence.** A certification is admissible in any proceeding under this Act, and a false certification is a violation of this Act by the covered entity and by the officer who signed it.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-7-2","heading":"\u00a7 7-2. Enforcement by the Commission","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Treatment.** A violation of this Act shall be treated as a violation of a rule defining an unfair or deceptive act or practice prescribed under section 18(a)(1)(B) of the Federal Trade Commission Act (15 U.S.C. 57a(a)(1)(B)), and the Commission shall enforce this Act in the same manner, by the same means, and with the same jurisdiction, powers, and duties as though the Federal Trade Commission Act were incorporated into this Act, except as this Section otherwise provides.\n\n**(b) Common carriers and nonprofit organizations.** Notwithstanding section 4, 5(a)(2), or 6 of the Federal Trade Commission Act or any jurisdictional limitation of the Commission, the Commission shall enforce this Act with respect to common carriers subject to the Communications Act of 1934 and to organizations not organized to carry on business for their own profit or that of their members.\n\n**(c) Own name.** Notwithstanding section 16(a) of the Federal Trade Commission Act, the Commission may commence, defend, and supervise the litigation of any action under this Act, and any appeal, in its own name, without referring the matter to the Attorney General.\n\n**(d) No rulemaking; no exemption.** The Commission has no authority to promulgate a rule under this Act, to exempt any person or practice from this Act, to narrow any definition in \u00a7 0-3, to add any purpose to \u00a7 1-2, or to approve any guideline, code, program, or certification that confers a presumption of compliance or a defense. Every obligation of this Act operates without any rule, guidance, designation, form, or adjudication by any agency.\n\n**(e) Disgorgement and deletion.** In an action by the Commission, a court may order disgorgement of the gain attributable to a violation, deletion of covered data collected, processed, retained, or transferred in violation of this Act, and the deletion under \u00a7 7-5 of any model trained on it."},{"anchor":"sec-7-3","heading":"\u00a7 7-3. Enforcement by a State","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** The attorney general of a State, and any officer or agency of a State authorized to enforce laws protecting the privacy of its residents, may bring a civil action in the name of the State or as parens patriae on behalf of its residents, in a district court of the United States or in a court of the State, to enjoin a violation of this Act, to enforce compliance, to obtain disgorgement, civil penalties as provided for a violation described in \u00a7 7-2(a), damages, restitution, deletion, and the relief under \u00a7 7-5, and to recover reasonable attorney's fees and costs.\n\n**(b)** The State shall notify the Commission at or before filing, and the Commission may intervene. Notice is not a precondition of the action, no waiting period applies, and the pendency of an action by the Commission bars a State only as to the violations alleged in the Commission's complaint against the same defendant.\n\n**(c)** This section supplements every other remedy under this Act and is a precondition of none."},{"anchor":"sec-7-4","heading":"\u00a7 7-4. Enforcement by the individual","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) The action.** An individual, or a class of individuals, may bring a civil action against a covered entity, a service provider, or a third party for a violation, as to that individual, of \u2014\n\n1. \u00a7 2-1, including a violation of \u00a7 2-2 or \u00a7 2-3 that results in targeting;\n2. \u00a7 2-5;\n3. \u00a7 2-6, \u00a7 4-3(a), or \u00a7 4-3(b);\n4. \u00a7 5-5(a), or chapter 119 of title 18, United States Code, as amended by Section 5; or\n5. \u00a7 4-2(c), as \u00a7 4-4(b) provides.\n\n**(b) No other duty.** No action under this section lies for a violation of Section 1, Section 3, Section 6, \u00a7 4-1, or \u00a7 7-1, the remedies for which are those \u00a7\u00a7 7-2 and 7-3 provide, and an injunction under subsection (d).\n\n**(c) Relief.** A prevailing individual shall be awarded, for each incident, the greater of the gain of the defendant attributable to the violation as it concerns the individual, and the amount set in the schedule to this Act; and a prevailing class the greater of the gain of the defendant attributable to the violation as it concerns the class, and that amount multiplied by the number of members of the class. An **incident** is a course of conduct in violation of one paragraph of subsection (a) as to one individual, however many acts it comprises and however long it continues, and **no amount accrues per act, per day, per record, per transfer, or per any unit other than the incident.** Gain is measured by the revenue the defendant received, or the cost it avoided, by reason of the violation, and the burden of allocating revenue to conduct other than the violation is on the defendant.\n\n**(d) Injunction; predicate facts.** A court shall enjoin a violation of any provision of this Act on the application of an individual affected by it. A violation of this Act constitutes irreparable injury to the individual whose covered data it concerns, for which there is no adequate remedy at law, and the balance of equities and the public interest favor relief. An action for an injunction only may be brought under this subsection for a violation of any Section of this Act, subject to subsection (f).\n\n**(e) Fees.** A prevailing individual or class shall be awarded reasonable attorney's fees, expert fees, and costs. A prevailing defendant may be awarded fees only where the action was frivolous, unreasonable, or without foundation. Fees shall not be reduced in proportion to the amount recovered, and a settlement conditioned on waiver of fees is unenforceable as to the fee award.\n\n**(f) Cure.** No opportunity to cure is a condition of an action for relief under subsection (c). An action for an injunction only, for a violation not within subsection (a), may be brought only where, not less than the period set in the schedule to this Act before filing, the individual gave the defendant written notice identifying the provision violated, and the violation continues; a defendant that has cured the violation and given the individual a written statement that it has been cured and will not recur may raise the cure as a defense to that action once, and not as to any later violation.\n\n**(g) Notice to public authorities.** An individual bringing an action under this section shall serve a copy of the complaint on the Commission and on the attorney general of the State in which the individual resides not later than the day of filing, and each may intervene as of right. Failure to serve is cured by serving, and is not a ground for dismissal or delay.\n\n**(h) Injury.** A violation described in subsection (a) as to an individual's covered data is a concrete and particularized injury in fact to that individual, is an invasion of an interest Congress has recognized, and bears a close relationship to the harms of intrusion upon seclusion and public disclosure of private facts.\n\n**(i) Expressly conferred.** The right and the action stated in this section are conferred expressly by this section, and no presumption against the creation of a private right or a private remedy applies to it.\n\n**(j) Concurrent jurisdiction; removal barred.** **(1)** An action under this section may be brought in a district court of the United States or in any State court of competent jurisdiction. **(2)** An action under this section brought in a State court may not be removed to a district court of the United States. **(3)** A State court shall not decline to hear an action under this section on the ground that it arises under Federal law, nor dismiss it on the ground that the plaintiff would lack standing in a court of the United States.\n\n**(k) No agency assignment.** Nothing in this Act authorizes a person to sue as an assignee, in whole or in part, of a claim of the United States.\n\n**(l) Predispute arbitration and joint-action waivers.** **(1)** Notwithstanding chapter 1 of title 9, United States Code, at the election of the individual, or of the named representative of a class action, no predispute arbitration agreement and no predispute joint-action waiver is valid or enforceable with respect to the case relating to a claim under this Act \u2014 and **case** means the entire civil proceeding, not the claim alone. The election may be made after the dispute arises, and a term purporting to require the election earlier, or to waive it, is void. **(2)** A court shall not compel arbitration of any part of such a case unless it first determines that this subsection does not apply, notwithstanding any delegation of that determination to an arbitrator and notwithstanding any incorporation of arbitral rules. **(3)** The election is available where the claim is pleaded nonfrivolously, and no more is required to invoke it. **(4)** Where any provision of an agreement is unenforceable under this subsection, the agreement is unenforceable in its entirety as to that case, notwithstanding any severability term in it. **(5)** An agreement is **predispute** if entered before the conduct giving rise to the claim occurred, whenever the claim is filed. **(6)** This subsection applies to an agreement entered before, on, or after the date of enactment. **(7)** Nothing in this subsection limits the operation of section 1 of title 9, United States Code, and the protections are cumulative.\n\n**(m) Forum and law.** A term selecting the forum or the law of another jurisdiction is void to the extent it would apply to a claim under this Act.\n\n**(n) Settlement preserved.** Subsection (l) does not affect an agreement settling an existing claim, made after the conduct giving rise to it occurred and identifying the claim it settles. An instrument that identifies no claim settles none.\n\n**(o) Pleading.** A complaint states a claim under this section where it alleges facts which, if true, would establish conduct within a paragraph of subsection (a) concerning the individual's covered data. No heightened standard of pleading applies; knowledge, intention, and motive may be alleged generally; and a plaintiff need not plead the absence of any exception, permitted purpose, or consent, each of which is a matter the defendant must plead and prove.\n\n**(p) Discovery before dismissal.** Where an element of a claim concerns what the defendant collected, processed, retained, or transferred, to whom, for what purpose, or with what consent, and the relevant records are within the defendant's control, a claim may not be dismissed before the plaintiff has had a reasonable opportunity to obtain them.\n\n**(q) Mootness.** The cessation, suspension, or withdrawal of a practice does not render an action moot where the defendant remains free to resume it; a defendant asserting mootness bears the burden of establishing that the practice cannot reasonably be expected to recur, and voluntary cessation after the commencement of an action does not satisfy that burden.\n\n**(r) Limitations.** An action under this section may be brought within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the later of the violation and the individual's discovery of it.\n\n**(s) Retaliation.** A covered entity may not deny, degrade, or price differently any product or service, or take any other adverse action, against an individual because the individual exercised a right or brought an action under this Act, and an individual so treated has an action under this section for the relief in subsection (c) as for an incident."},{"anchor":"sec-7-5","heading":"\u00a7 7-5. Deletion of a model","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** In an action under \u00a7 7-2, \u00a7 7-3, or \u00a7 7-4, where a court finds that covered data was collected, processed, retained, or transferred in violation of Section 1 or Section 2, and that a model, algorithm, or system was trained, fine-tuned, or evaluated on that data, the court shall order the deletion of the model and of every derivative of it, and shall order the defendant to identify every person to which the model or its outputs were transferred, unless the defendant establishes that the model can be, and will within a period the court sets be, retrained without the data and without any product of it.\n\n**(b)** The deletion of a model under this section is a consequence of the violation and not a measure of damages, and no showing of harm from the model is required."},{"anchor":"sec-7-6","heading":"\u00a7 7-6. Defenses, construction, and procedure","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a) Defenses exclusive.** The defenses, exceptions, permitted purposes, and limitations on liability available in an action under this Act are those and only those expressly stated in it. The common law of the United States or of any State supplies none. No defense is to be inferred from the omission of any subject from this Act, and the absence of a provision addressing a defense recognized at common law is a determination by Congress that it is not available.\n\n**(b) No reconstitution.** A limitation omitted from this Act may not be reintroduced as an element of a plaintiff's case, a pleading requirement, a standard of proof, a limitation on remedy, or a prudential limitation on jurisdiction.\n\n**(c) No channeling.** No administrative remedy is a prerequisite to an action under this Act, and no statute establishing a remedial scheme displaces a remedy provided by this Act unless it so provides by express reference to this Act.\n\n**(d) Remedial; canons displaced.** This Act is remedial and shall be construed to give effect to its remedial purpose. Congress has considered and expressly displaces, as to this Act, the presumption against the creation of causes of action, the presumption against extraterritorial application to the extent \u00a7 4-5 provides, and any presumption that a remedial scheme enacted elsewhere displaces a remedy provided here. Each cause of action created by this Act is created by this Act, and no court may decline to entertain it on the ground that special factors counsel hesitation, that an alternative remedial structure exists, or that the context is new.\n\n**(e) Causation.** Liability under this Act requires that the conduct concern the individual's covered data. No showing that the individual was harmed, deceived, or made worse off is an element of any violation, and no other causal standard applies.\n\n**(f) Good faith is not a defense.** The good faith of a covered entity, its reliance on a contract, a representation, a certification, or a guideline, and its ignorance of the source of covered data are not defenses to a violation of Section 2, Section 4, or Section 5."},{"anchor":"sec-7-7","heading":"\u00a7 7-7. Character of amounts received","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"An amount received by an individual under \u00a7 7-4 that returns a payment the individual made is a return of that payment and is not income. Any other amount received under \u00a7 7-4 is includible in gross income but is not wages and is not net earnings from self-employment. An amount received by an individual for attorney's fees, expert fees, and costs under \u00a7 7-4 is not included in the individual's gross income. The deletion of data or of a model under this Act is not a discharge of indebtedness and gives rise to no income to any person. No amount paid under \u00a7 7-2(e), \u00a7 7-3, or \u00a7 7-4, other than an award of fees and costs, is deductible under any provision of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, and no such amount is restitution or remediation for purposes of section 162(f)(2) of that Code."},{"anchor":"sec-7-8","heading":"\u00a7 7-8. Conforming amendments","part":"SECTION 7 \u2014 ENFORCEMENT","body":"**(a)** Section 1445 of title 28, United States Code, is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(g)** A civil action arising under section 7-4 of the Personal Privacy Act may not be removed to any district court of the United States.\"\n\n**(b)** Section 62(e) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(24)** The Personal Privacy Act.\"\n\n**(c) Operative in any event.** Section 7-4(j)(2) operates according to its terms as an Act of Congress expressly providing otherwise within the meaning of section 1441(a) of title 28, United States Code, and \u00a7 7-7 operates according to its terms, whether or not the amendments made by this section take effect."},{"anchor":"sec-8-1","heading":"\u00a7 8-1. No agency action is required","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Every obligation and prohibition in this Act operates without any rulemaking, guidance, designation, form, adjudication, or other action by any agency or officer of the United States, and the absence of any such action does not suspend, toll, or diminish any right or prohibition under this Act.\n\n**(b)** No agency is authorized to exempt a person or a practice from this Act, to narrow a definition in \u00a7 0-3, to add a purpose to \u00a7 1-2, or to add an exception to \u00a7 6-3.","partHeads":[{"heading":"SECTION 8 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":""}]},{"anchor":"sec-8-2","heading":"\u00a7 8-2. Relationship to other law","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Floor, not ceiling.** This Act does not preempt a State, local, or Tribal law that affords an individual greater protection, and nothing in this Act limits any right or remedy under such a law. A State law is not preempted by reason of addressing a subject this Act addresses.\n\n**(b) Other Federal privacy law.** To the extent a person is required to comply, as to an activity, with title V of the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act; part C of title XI of the Social Security Act and the regulations under section 264(c) of the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996; the Fair Credit Reporting Act; section 444 of the General Education Provisions Act; part 46 of title 45, Code of Federal Regulations; section 543 of the Public Health Service Act; or the Children's Online Privacy Protection Act of 1998, that activity is governed by that law and not by Section 1 or Section 6 of this Act; Sections 2, 4, and 5 apply to the person and the activity in any event. No provision of this Act relieves any obligation under any other Federal law.\n\n**(c) Communications Act preserved.** Nothing in this Act limits the authority of the Federal Communications Commission under sections 222, 338(i), or 631 of the Communications Act of 1934, or any regulation under them, and where such a provision and this Act both apply the more protective governs.\n\n**(d) No implied repeal.** This Act does not limit any other right or remedy, and compliance with this Act is not a defense to a claim under any other law.\n\n**(e) Commercial messages.** A permission to send a commercial message under the CAN-SPAM Act of 2003 or section 227 of the Communications Act of 1934, or under any regulation under either, does not permit a communication \u00a7 2-1 prohibits, and compliance with either is not a defense to a violation of this Act; where either and this Act both apply, the more protective governs."},{"anchor":"sec-8-3","heading":"\u00a7 8-3. Severability","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder and every other application remain in force.\n\n**(b) By section and by rule.** Each section of this Act is severable from each other section, and each prohibition stated in Section 2 is severable from each other prohibition. The application of \u00a7 2-1 to an advertising facilitator is severable from its application to an advertiser; the application of \u00a7 2-5 to a vehicle is severable from its application to every other device; and the application of any provision to a person outside the United States is severable from its application to every other person.\n\n**(c) No integrated scheme.** Nothing in this Act shall be cited as evidence that its provisions constitute a single design. Each prohibition, each duty, and each remedy rests on its own authority and operates whether or not any other is available or valid."},{"anchor":"sec-8-4","heading":"\u00a7 8-4. Resources and continuity of operations","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a) Permanent appropriation for the ministerial core.** There are appropriated, out of any money in the Treasury not otherwise appropriated, such sums as are necessary for the registry and mechanism under \u00a7 4-2 and for the duties under \u00a7 7-1(a). Amounts under this subsection are appropriated permanently and indefinitely, are available without further appropriation and without fiscal-year limitation, and do not lapse.\n\n**(b) Authorization for enforcement.** There are authorized to be appropriated to the Commission, for each fiscal year, such sums as are necessary for the enforcement of this Act, and amounts so appropriated are available until expended.\n\n**(c) Continuity during a lapse in appropriations.** The maintenance of the registry, the operation of the mechanism under \u00a7 4-2, and the receipt and publication of registrations and certifications are activities authorized by law to continue during a lapse in appropriations, and obligations incurred for them are authorized in advance of appropriations for purposes of section 1342 of title 31, United States Code. The officers and employees necessary to perform them shall be designated as excepted from furlough, identified in the Commission's shutdown plan, and that identification published.\n\n**(d) No duty is excused by funding.** No duty this Act makes ministerial is excused, tolled, suspended, or rendered ineffective by the absence or insufficiency of an appropriation, by a failure to request one, or by a failure to apportion, allot, or obligate amounts appropriated, and a defense that the duty was not effective for want of funds is not available in any action to enforce it.\n\n**(e) Deadlines.** A deadline this Act imposes on a private person to act through the registry or the mechanism is tolled for the duration of a lapse in appropriations affecting the Commission, and for 14 days thereafter; a deadline imposed on a data broker under \u00a7 4-2(c) is not tolled where the request was delivered under \u00a7 4-2(f), or where the request is expedited under \u00a7 4-2(b-1)."},{"anchor":"sec-8-5","heading":"\u00a7 8-5. Effective dates","part":"SECTION 8 \u2014 GENERAL PROVISIONS","body":"**(a)** Sections 0, 7-2(d), 8-1, 8-2, 8-3, and 8-4 take effect on the date of enactment.\n\n**(b)** Sections 1, 2, 3, 5, and 6, and \u00a7 4-3, take effect on the date set in the schedule to this Act after enactment, and apply to covered data collected before, on, or after that date and to a consent, term, or setting given or made before, on, or after that date.\n\n**(c)** Section 4-1 takes effect on the date set in the schedule to this Act after enactment, and \u00a7\u00a7 4-2 and 7-1 on the date set in the schedule to this Act after enactment; \u00a7 4-2(f) applies from the date \u00a7 4-1 takes effect.\n\n**(d)** Section 7-4 takes effect, as to each provision it enforces, on the date that provision takes effect."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Radius for precise geolocation** (\u00a7 0-3(c)(6)): [___ feet]\n2. **Data broker threshold, individuals per 12 months** (\u00a7 0-3(k)): [___ individuals]\n3. **Period before a refused or revoked consent may be requested again** (\u00a7 1-3(c), \u00a7 2-7(a)(3), \u00a7 3-1(e), \u00a7 3-2): [___ months]\n4. **Period to stop processing and delete on revocation** (\u00a7 1-3(d)): [___ days]\n5. **Biometric retention limit after last interaction** (\u00a7 1-4(b)): [___ years]\n6. **Minimum cohort size for an aggregate report** (\u00a7 2-3(b)(1)): [___ individuals or devices]\n7. **Minimum delay before an aggregate report** (\u00a7 2-3(b)(2)): [___ hours]\n8. **Minimum interval between reports on overlapping cohorts** (\u00a7 2-3(b)(4)): [___ hours]\n9. **Period to honor a request to stop communications** (\u00a7 2-4(a)(4)): [___ days]\n10. **Period to delete captured sensor data after the function is performed** (\u00a7 2-5(c)): [___ days]\n11. **Period to de-identify sensor data retained for service improvement** (\u00a7 2-5(e)): [___ days]\n12. **Date by which a data broker must first register** (\u00a7 4-1(a)): [___ days after \u00a7 4-1 takes effect]\n13. **Period for the Commission to publish a registration or certification** (\u00a7 4-2(a), \u00a7 7-1(a)(2)): [___ days]\n14. **Maximum interval at which a data broker must retrieve requests** (\u00a7 4-2(c)): [___ days]\n15. **Period for a data broker to comply with a request** (\u00a7 4-2(c)): [___ days]\n16. **Daily amount for failure to register, and the 30-day escalation** (\u00a7 4-4(a)): [$___ per day; increasing by $___ each 30 days]\n17. **Daily amount owed to the individual for failure to delete** (\u00a7 4-4(b)): [$___ per day]\n18. **Period to comply with or decline a rights request** (\u00a7 6-2(a)): [___ days]\n19. **Requests per year without charge** (\u00a7 6-2(b)): [___ requests]\n20. **Period to decide an appeal** (\u00a7 6-2(e)): [___ days]\n21. **Certification threshold, individuals per calendar year** (\u00a7 7-1(b)): [___ individuals]\n22. **Certification filing date** (\u00a7 7-1(b)): [___ of each year]\n23. **Statutory amount per individual per incident** (\u00a7 7-4(c)): [$___]\n24. **Notice period before an injunction-only action for a duty outside \u00a7 7-4(a)** (\u00a7 7-4(f)): [___ days]\n25. **Limitations period** (\u00a7 7-4(r)): [___ years]\n26. **Effective date of the substantive Sections named in \u00a7 8-5(b)** (\u00a7 8-5(b)): [___ months after enactment]\n27. **Effective date of \u00a7 4-1** (\u00a7 8-5(c)): [___ months after enactment]\n28. **Effective date of \u00a7\u00a7 4-2 and 7-1** (\u00a7 8-5(c)): [___ months after enactment]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-12"}}}};
